---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Demo from "../components/Demo.astro";
import BaselineStrip from "../components/BaselineStrip.astro";
import Predict from "../components/Predict.astro";
import Num from "../components/Num.astro";
import { leaderboard, generation, vocab, POPULARITY, n, href, bootstrap, countIsExact, stability } from "../lib/data";

const top = vocab.by_frequency.slice(0, 3).map((v) => v.name.replace(/_/g, " "));

/* Counted over exported embeddings, which is the larger and more defensible
 * number for a claim about vectors. The headline uses the smaller one because
 * it scores each system at its best available rule; both appear above the
 * fold so the difference comes from us rather than from a reviewer. */
const belowEmb = leaderboard.models.filter(
  (m) => (m.M6_recall_at_10 ?? m.best_recall_at_10) < POPULARITY).length;

const WORDS = ["Zero", "One", "Two", "Three", "Four", "Five", "Six", "Seven",
  "Eight", "Nine", "Ten", "Eleven", "Twelve", "Thirteen", "Fourteen",
  "Fifteen", "Sixteen"];
const nWord = (v: number) => WORDS[v] ?? String(v);
---

<Base
  title={`We trained ${leaderboard.n_models} ingredient models on ${n(generation.recipes)} recipes. ${nWord(leaderboard.n_below_popularity)} lost to counting.`}
  chapter="One — the claim"
  standfirst={`A sorted frequency table beat ${belowEmb} of the ${leaderboard.n_models} exported embeddings. Letting every system use its own strongest scoring rule rescued exactly two.`}
>
  <div class="wide-figure">
    <Predict />
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="muted small">
      {n(bootstrap!.n_instances)} held-out completions ·{" "}
      {stability ? `${stability.seeds.length} training seeds · ` : ""}
      {n(bootstrap!.n_boot)} bootstrap resamples ·{" "}
      <a href={href("methods/")}>methods</a>,{" "}
      <a href={href("trust/")}>controls</a> and{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/incrediblecrab/llmmm">code</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Now play the same test</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Here is the test the models took. Take a recipe, hide one ingredient,
      and name it from what remains. You get one guess; the machines get ten,
      from {n(vocab.n)} candidates. Play a few rounds before you read any
      further — the rest of this site is easier to believe once you have lost
      a round yourself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Demo />

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="muted small">
      Recipes are drawn from the held-out set, so nothing scoring them was
      trained on them. EASE runs in your browser on its own item-item matrix,
      the same arithmetic the evaluation harness ran; re-scored from these
      exact bytes it reaches recall@10 of{" "}
      <Num value={0.5871} /> against <Num value={0.5872} /> at full precision.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wide-figure hero">
    <BaselineStrip />
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Almost every system that recommends things learns a vector for each item
      and puts similar items near each other. It is the standard move. It is
      also, for this problem, frequently worse than counting.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ranking by raw frequency — no learning, no vectors, just a sorted table
      of how often each ingredient appears — recovers the hidden ingredient{" "}
      <Num value={POPULARITY} pct dp={1} tone="accent" /> of the time.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the line to beat, and it is not a strawman. It suggests{" "}
      {top[0]}, then {top[1]}, then {top[2]}. Recipes really are mostly made
      of those.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the models did</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Sixteen models, spanning matrix factorisation, graph neural networks,
      word2vec-style objectives, autoencoders and a set transformer. Each
      trained on the same corpus, each evaluated on the same held-out recipes,
      each scored against the same frequency table.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>{leaderboard.n_below_popularity} of {leaderboard.n_models} finish
      below the line.</b> Not marginally — several are closer to zero than to
      the baseline.
      {bootstrap && countIsExact() && (
        <>
          {" "}Resampling the
          {" "}{n(bootstrap.n_instances)} completion instances
          {" "}{n(bootstrap.n_boot)} times returns the same count of
          {" "}{leaderboard.n_below_popularity} every single time, so the
          {" "}headline is not an artefact of which recipes were drawn. Whether
          {" "}it survives retraining is
          {" "}<a href={href("robustness/")}>a separate question</a>.
        </>
      )}
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="headline-stat">
    <div class="figure-row">
      <Num value={leaderboard.n_below_popularity} dp={0} size="display" tone="below" />
      <p class="what">
        of the {leaderboard.n_models} models score below a ranked frequency
        table at finishing a recipe.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="scale">
      Trained on <Num value={generation.recipes} dp={0} /> recipes,{" "}
      <Num value={generation.slots} dp={0} /> ingredient slots, a vocabulary
      of <Num value={vocab.n} dp={0} group /> ingredients.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The next section shows the whole table, with the baseline drawn where it
      actually falls rather than appended at the bottom.
    </p>
    <p class="next">
      <a href={href("/models/")}
        >Continue: the models →</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .small { font-size: var(--t-sm); }

  /* One number, set as the subject of a sentence it is actually part of.
   * This was four figures of equal weight in a grid, which is the shape a
   * dashboard uses when it has not decided what matters — and at seven and
   * eight digits two of them overflowed their tracks and overlapped. Only
   * the finding is set large now; the corpus figures are provenance and
   * read as a caption, which is what they are. */
  .headline-stat {
    margin-block: var(--s-7);
    padding-top: var(--s-5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .figure-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }
  .figure-row .what {
    font-size: var(--t-lg);
    line-height: 1.25;
    max-width: 20rem;
    margin: 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .scale {
    margin-top: var(--s-4);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
  }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .figure-row { flex-direction: column; gap: var(--s-2); }
  }

  .next { margin-top: var(--s-6); font-size: var(--t-md); }
  .next a { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; }
</style>
